From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nate Isern <nate@oxy.so>
Date: Mon, 11 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Serve astro://management from Astro

Swaps upstream's `ManagementUIConfig` for `astro::AstroManagementUIConfig`,
under upstream's own host. A SWAP rather than an addition because
`WebUIConfigMap::AddWebUIConfigImpl` CHECKs on a duplicate origin, so a second
registration for the same origin aborts the browser at startup.

WHY THIS HOST IS SAFE TO TAKE OVER, when the reading-list panel's is not. A
registration swap hands the browser a different controller CLASS for the same
host, so anything outside the config that names the concrete type stops
working -- and `WebUIController::GetAs<T>()` returns nullptr on a type mismatch
while the call sites in the views layer routinely dereference it unchecked,
which makes that a browser-process crash rather than a page that fails to
load. Measured across the surfaces on the takeover list: `ManagementUI` is
named in ZERO files under `chrome/browser/ui/views/`, against three for
`ReadingListUI` (read_later_side_panel_web_view.{h,cc}) and two for `IntroUI`
(first_run_flow_controller.cc, one of them behind a DCHECK, so a release build
dereferences null where a developer build asserts). Downloads, history,
bookmarks and feedback are also zero.

The Astro controller adopts `ManagementUIHandler` wholesale and unmodified, the
same move the settings page makes with fifteen upstream handlers: what it
answers is enterprise policy state, and reimplementing it would be
reimplementing the enterprise management surface.

What the page stops doing is the point of doing this at all. Upstream's
management page, measured on the shipped binary with an unmanaged profile,
hands the document a `browserManagementNotice` that is not a sentence but
HTML -- a whole `<a target="_blank" href="https://support.google.com/chrome?p=
is_chrome_managed">Learn more</a>` element, injected -- and it says "Activity
on this device may be managed outside of Chromium" in a browser called Astro.
Beside it sits a promotion banner whose button opens
`https://admin.google.com/ac/chrome/guides/?ref=browser&utm_source=chrome_policy_cec`
and reports two interaction events about the click. The Astro page states the
same fact in its own words, with no link off the machine and no banner, and
declares `connect-src 'none'`.

Owned by https://github.com/OxyHQ/Astro/issues/15.
---
diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/webui/chrome_web_ui_configs.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/webui/chrome_web_ui_configs.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/webui/chrome_web_ui_configs.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/webui/chrome_web_ui_configs.cc
@@ -9,4 +9,5 @@
 #include "chrome/browser/oxy/webui/astro_adblock_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/oxy/webui/astro_alia_ui.h"
+#include "chrome/browser/oxy/webui/astro_management_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/oxy/webui/astro_ntp_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/oxy/webui/astro_settings_ui.h"
@@ -344,4 +345,4 @@ void RegisterChromeWebUIConfigs() {
   map.AddWebUIConfig(std::make_unique<LegionInternalsUIConfig>());
-  map.AddWebUIConfig(std::make_unique<ManagementUIConfig>());
+  map.AddWebUIConfig(std::make_unique<astro::AstroManagementUIConfig>());
   map.AddWebUIConfig(
       std::make_unique<media_router::MediaRouterInternalsUIConfig>());
